<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_ampere" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Ampère's Law</title>
		
    <corollary xml:id="vid-ampere">
	<title>Ampère's Law</title>
	
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>

		<video youtube="gqR1NwRBWiI"><description>Introducing Ampere's Law.</description></video>

	</statement>
  </corollary>

  <subsubsection><title>Key Ideas</title></subsubsection>
	
	<definition>
		<title>Ampère's Law</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p><term>Ampère's Law</term> relates the magnetic field around a closed curve to the current passing through the region bounded by the curve: <me>\oint_S \vec{B}\cdot d\vec{l} = \mu_0 I_{enclosed}</me></p>
				<p>
					This is a line integral, like the integral for <xref ref="e_to_v"/>. The circle over the integral symbol indicates that this is an integral over a <em>closed path</em>: when this is true, the line integral is known as the <term>circulation</term> of the magnetic field.
				</p>
		</statement>
	</definition>

<p>
		Ampère's Law (like Gauss's Law) is extremely useful when your current distribution has just the right symmetry. To use Ampère's Law effectively, you need:
		<ol>
			<li>
				<p>
					the magnetic field to be parallel or perpendicular to the path <m>d\vec{l}</m>
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					the magnetic field to be uniform along the path
				</p>
			</li>
		</ol>
		These conditions will simplify the dot product and the integral, allowing the magnitude of the magnetic field to be pulled out of the integral.
	</p>

	<subsubsection><title>Example Derivation: Infinite Wire</title></subsubsection>
<p>
		A straightforward application of Ampère's Law is to confirm that the magnetic field created by a very long wire carrying constant current <m>I</m> is
		
		<me>\vec{B} = \frac{\mu_0}{2\pi}\frac{\vec{I} \times \hat{s}}{s}</me>

		The system exhibits cylindrical symmetry: that is, the magnetic field only depends on the distance <m>s</m> away from the axis where the wire lies, and it points only in the <m>\hat{\phi}</m> direction.  As a result, a circle of radius <m>s</m> is a natural choice for the Amperian loop.

		The total current flowing through this loop is exactly <m>I</m>, so all that is left to calculate is the line integral:

		<me>\oint_S \vec{B}\cdot d\vec{l} = \int_0^{2\pi} \left( B_\phi \hat{\phi} \right) \cdot \left( s d\phi \hat{\phi} \right) = B_\phi s \int_0^{2\pi}d\phi = 2\pi s B_\phi</me>

		Here <m>B_\phi</m> represents the magnitude of the magnetic field's circumferential component (the component that points <em>around</em> the wire).  The other components of the magnetic field are zero.

		Relating this to the current via Ampère's Law results in the correct magnitude of the magnetic field!

		<me>B_\phi = \frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi s}</me>

		</p>

	
	<subsubsection>
		<title>Activities</title>
		<investigation>
      <title>Three Loops</title>
      <p></p>
			<p>Shown below are three different cases, each with two identical current-carrying wires and a circular Amperian loop.  In which case is the circulation of the magnetic field around the loop the largest?</p>
			<figure xml:id="x_fig_ampere">
			<title>Amperian Loop Figure</title>
				<caption>Three cases are shown depicting an Amperian loop (dashed line) and different wires.</caption>
				<image source="mag/Ampo.png" width = "100%" ><description>Three boxes sit side by side. The lefthand box is case A, where one wire sits inside a closed Amperian loop and another wire sits outside the loop. The middle box shows case B, where both wires sit outside the loop. The righthand box shows case C where both wires sit inside the loop.</description></image>
			</figure>
		</investigation>
	</subsubsection>

<!--NO AI-->
</section>

